1
00:00:13,686 --> 00:00:14,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever that object is.

2
00:00:15,966 --> 00:00:31,950
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I start doing that, I'll lose the sight of object of fear, where I'll notice the grief being lessened, like it's ceasing.

3
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:39,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But am I paying attention to the object of fear anymore?

4
00:00:39,371 --> 00:00:39,551
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

5
00:00:39,871 --> 00:00:42,332
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm paying attention to more about the grief.

6
00:00:45,052 --> 00:00:54,404
[SPEAKER_00]: As I mentioned before, whatever you do, you shouldn't be... it should not be rooted in your desire to get rid of the disagreeable aspect of that fear.

7
00:00:56,727 --> 00:01:02,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you could be... because you could be paying attention to the fear, but you're not trying to get rid of it.

8
00:01:05,437 --> 00:01:06,577
[SPEAKER_01]: I am, but then I stop.

9
00:01:06,597 --> 00:01:07,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, that's what I mean.

10
00:01:07,318 --> 00:01:07,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it's a problem.

11
00:01:08,318 --> 00:01:13,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can also not be paying attention to fear, pay attention to something else, because you want to get rid of it.

12
00:01:13,439 --> 00:01:14,160
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a problem.

13
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:16,820
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem is not where you pay your attention.

14
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,181
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is whether you're trying to get rid of the arisen experience or not.

15
00:01:20,361 --> 00:01:22,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're overstepping your mark.

16
00:01:23,922 --> 00:01:25,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it has arisen on its own.

17
00:01:26,252 --> 00:01:30,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to train in regard to the arisen phenomena that's enduring for you.

18
00:01:30,453 --> 00:01:36,514
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to be replacing the phenomena, or trying to get rid of it, or prolong it, or get the better one instead.

19
00:01:36,714 --> 00:01:37,674
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not on you.

20
00:01:40,495 --> 00:01:47,877
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you're focusing on the fear, whether you're losing the sight of the object of the fear, but looking at the grief in regard to the fear, that's all fine.

21
00:01:47,897 --> 00:01:50,797
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all part of the same arisen experience of disagreeable.

22
00:01:51,957 --> 00:01:52,918
[SPEAKER_00]: In this case, fear.

23
00:01:53,778 --> 00:01:54,278
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like...

24
00:01:56,359 --> 00:02:00,882
[SPEAKER_01]: The pain or the painfulness of the feeling is still there.

25
00:02:01,102 --> 00:02:01,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

26
00:02:01,663 --> 00:02:04,124
[SPEAKER_00]: The disagreeable aspect of the pain is still there.

27
00:02:04,484 --> 00:02:08,627
[SPEAKER_00]: If you stop resisting it, the disagreeable aspect will stop being painful.

28
00:02:09,308 --> 00:02:10,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it's just displeasing.

29
00:02:11,269 --> 00:02:11,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

30
00:02:12,029 --> 00:02:15,271
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's easy to withhold.

31
00:02:15,432 --> 00:02:15,892
[SPEAKER_00]: No problem.

32
00:02:16,332 --> 00:02:17,313
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot overwhelm you.

33
00:02:18,413 --> 00:02:20,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's when you want to get rid of

34
00:02:21,463 --> 00:02:26,406
[SPEAKER_00]: The disagreeable aspect, when you basically, when you crave in regard to it, that's why you suffer.

35
00:02:27,106 --> 00:02:27,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

36
00:02:27,707 --> 00:02:29,388
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't suffer because of the disagreeable.

37
00:02:30,048 --> 00:02:34,411
[SPEAKER_00]: You suffer because of your craving of trying to get rid of the disagreeable.

38
00:02:34,711 --> 00:02:34,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

39
00:02:35,351 --> 00:02:45,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if, like, when I see that I'm craving to get rid of it, but then I see that I'm craving to get rid of it, so I stop, take a step back and I

40
00:02:46,755 --> 00:02:48,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop trying to get rid of it, basically.

41
00:02:48,077 --> 00:02:48,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

42
00:02:48,738 --> 00:02:54,146
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, that's how, you know, when you hear in this, like, generic spiritual teachings, just, just accept everything.

43
00:02:54,526 --> 00:02:59,013
[SPEAKER_00]: There is, like, it works in that initial manner for people, accepting everything.

44
00:03:01,456 --> 00:03:05,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, in order for it to work fundamentally, you need to understand what it is.

45
00:03:05,980 --> 00:03:13,187
[SPEAKER_00]: But the reason why it works, it's like a like a blanket acceptance, works because to the degree that they were resisting it, they stopped resisting it.

46
00:03:13,648 --> 00:03:15,830
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it does provide that a little relief.

47
00:03:16,510 --> 00:03:21,635
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, as a view of accepting everything, it's wrong, because there are certain things that you overly accept.

48
00:03:22,676 --> 00:03:26,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the attitude of just accept everything was going to double down your accepting.

49
00:03:27,407 --> 00:03:33,468
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, things that are pleasing, things that are agreeable, things that you actually want more than they actually are.

50
00:03:34,689 --> 00:03:44,351
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the view, accept everything is wrong, but it works relatively when people resist something, and then they accept it, through that, even through that wrong view.

51
00:03:44,391 --> 00:03:48,332
[SPEAKER_00]: To that degree, they were resisting, they stopped resisting, and to that degree, they experience relief.

52
00:03:49,905 --> 00:03:57,267
[SPEAKER_00]: But really, you want to accept things that you've been... well, accept things to the extent that you've been resisting them, but not more than that.

53
00:03:59,948 --> 00:04:09,912
[SPEAKER_00]: And you also want to resist things that you've been accepting, only to the extent that you've been accepting them, but not more than that.

54
00:04:11,792 --> 00:04:16,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, it's not something... it comes down to training your mind, as I just explained.

55
00:04:16,554 --> 00:04:19,715
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not something you can, like, catch in one moment or two, but

56
00:04:20,081 --> 00:04:27,625
[SPEAKER_00]: By... by paying attention to that which is... which your mind wants and which your mind doesn't want.

57
00:04:28,045 --> 00:04:37,930
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say paying attention, I mean like being mindful of, not trying to attend every second of it, but be mindful of there is an experience enduring, sometimes for a whole day.

58
00:04:37,970 --> 00:04:41,612
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have a whole same mood present in the morning, in the evening, for five days.

59
00:04:41,672 --> 00:04:42,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

60
00:04:43,993 --> 00:04:49,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to be discerning that and then stop resisting it on that peripheral general background level.

61
00:04:50,287 --> 00:04:56,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you're in a bad mood, say, disagreeable... Like, bad mood is bad only because you're resisting the disagreeable aspect of it.

62
00:04:57,835 --> 00:05:00,038
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's a disagreeable... And then, why is it disagreeable?

63
00:05:01,019 --> 00:05:01,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't want it.

64
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:03,662
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you keep fueling it in itself.

65
00:05:06,282 --> 00:05:07,843
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to... but I want the pain.

66
00:05:07,883 --> 00:05:08,063
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

67
00:05:08,143 --> 00:05:12,487
[SPEAKER_00]: You just have to stop resisting it, because it's nothing to do with you to begin with.

68
00:05:13,067 --> 00:05:20,052
[SPEAKER_00]: And that can become apparent if you give... if you... if you're able to create some space for your mind to move around.

69
00:05:21,073 --> 00:05:23,415
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the view is the most important thing.

70
00:05:23,835 --> 00:05:33,503
[SPEAKER_00]: If your view is, I must accept everything, or I must deny everything, or I must look at everything, attend to everything, one, two, three, four, not miss a moment, not miss a moment, there is no space there to move, there is no space to reflect.

71
00:05:34,658 --> 00:05:40,061
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're acting as if, as I said, as if your own mind is your own.

72
00:05:41,281 --> 00:05:49,585
[SPEAKER_00]: If your own mind were your own, as I said, a simple decision, a determination to do something would be enough for you to do it, or to stop doing it.

73
00:05:50,626 --> 00:05:52,547
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have a full say on the way

74
00:05:53,372 --> 00:05:54,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind works.

75
00:05:54,552 --> 00:05:57,333
[SPEAKER_00]: All you have to do is say no once, and that will be cemented.

76
00:05:57,793 --> 00:06:02,435
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't experience discrepancy, you wouldn't experience the pressure, you wouldn't... you wouldn't need to restrain yourself.

77
00:06:02,495 --> 00:06:04,615
[SPEAKER_00]: But your mind has a mind on its own.

78
00:06:04,715 --> 00:06:06,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind is a separate animal.

79
00:06:06,076 --> 00:06:07,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind is a sixth sense.

80
00:06:08,496 --> 00:06:09,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't listen to you.

81
00:06:09,897 --> 00:06:11,097
[SPEAKER_00]: So you want to train it.

82
00:06:15,238 --> 00:06:18,079
[SPEAKER_00]: And you train it by teaching it right from wrong.

83
00:06:18,793 --> 00:06:33,626
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if you keep interfering and yanking its chain, even when it's not doing anything that's rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, it's not doing anything that's wrong, yet you keep yanking it, then it will think that non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion is also wrong.

84
00:06:33,646 --> 00:06:35,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you keep punishing it for whatever it does.

85
00:06:36,569 --> 00:06:38,330
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how meditation techniques work.

86
00:06:38,971 --> 00:06:41,573
[SPEAKER_00]: People just keep interfering with everything.

87
00:06:42,351 --> 00:06:48,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Through the observance of the meditation technique, there's watching, in-breath, out-breath, in-breath, out-breath, just mechanical.

88
00:06:49,976 --> 00:06:52,397
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like you're mechanically trying to train an animal.

89
00:06:52,557 --> 00:06:53,418
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't work like that.

90
00:06:55,579 --> 00:06:58,221
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the simile of the bull, as I started speaking.

91
00:06:58,281 --> 00:06:59,161
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to just finish it now.

92
00:06:59,982 --> 00:07:06,726
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you... your bull is just wild, goes everywhere, eats other people's grass, other people's fruit, and that's a problem.

93
00:07:07,349 --> 00:07:13,332
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you bring it down to your own ground, where it can be, where it can move around, where it can find the food.

94
00:07:15,253 --> 00:07:20,796
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you keep an eye on it, just so that it stays on your ground.

95
00:07:22,777 --> 00:07:28,360
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't allow it to go further when it's not supposed to, where you will lose the sight of it, lose control of it.

96
00:07:29,196 --> 00:07:34,100
[SPEAKER_00]: But what people do, is people chain the bull, and then, if the bull moves left, they pull it right.

97
00:07:34,140 --> 00:07:35,340
[SPEAKER_00]: If it moves right, they pull it left.

98
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:37,962
[SPEAKER_00]: They interfere with every thought, with everything that arises.

99
00:07:38,823 --> 00:07:40,444
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, that bull doesn't know what to do.

100
00:07:41,505 --> 00:07:43,346
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep attacking it for whatever it does.

101
00:07:44,167 --> 00:07:46,489
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it cannot differentiate good from bad.

102
00:07:46,969 --> 00:07:50,772
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you let that bull, say he moves to the left, but it's still within your field, it's fine.

103
00:07:51,592 --> 00:07:52,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Moves to the right, it's it.

104
00:07:52,713 --> 00:07:54,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you realize, okay, I can move here, I can move here.

105
00:07:55,556 --> 00:07:57,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it goes a step further into another.

106
00:07:57,237 --> 00:07:59,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when you hit it, then you realize, ah, okay.

107
00:07:59,740 --> 00:08:02,102
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not if I move left or if I move right.

108
00:08:02,182 --> 00:08:04,524
[SPEAKER_00]: It's how far left and how far right I go.

109
00:08:05,525 --> 00:08:08,568
[SPEAKER_00]: And eventually, it learns and eventually, you will know.

110
00:08:09,188 --> 00:08:10,430
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to look at it anymore.

111
00:08:10,610 --> 00:08:13,953
[SPEAKER_00]: It will not go where I trained it to not go.

112
00:08:16,095 --> 00:08:16,856
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean, like,

113
00:08:17,963 --> 00:08:25,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Even people's meditation practice should revolve around discernment, peripheral mindfulness, from which you have the perspective.

114
00:08:25,766 --> 00:08:26,727
[SPEAKER_00]: No, this is still fine.

115
00:08:26,747 --> 00:08:28,527
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no greed, aversion, delusion involved.

116
00:08:28,667 --> 00:08:29,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop trying to interfere.

117
00:08:29,868 --> 00:08:32,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop trying to stop thinking, or whatever else people do today.

118
00:08:34,250 --> 00:08:35,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Let it endure.

119
00:08:35,970 --> 00:08:38,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Vitāka-vicāra means thinking and pondering.

120
00:08:41,392 --> 00:08:45,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Means there is space for thoughts to move, to arise, to present themselves.

121
00:08:47,332 --> 00:08:51,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Um, actually, about that, um, do you have a Manjumanikā here?

122
00:08:51,554 --> 00:08:52,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Right next to me.

123
00:08:53,634 --> 00:08:57,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Yesterday, I came across something, um, here.

124
00:09:00,017 --> 00:09:00,397
[SPEAKER_00]: What's that?

125
00:09:01,557 --> 00:09:12,461
[SPEAKER_01]: He, um, if you go through this paragraph, it it basically talks about, uh, a little bit of saying that, um, thinking and pondering for too long can be tiresome for your body.

126
00:09:13,941 --> 00:09:15,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there are two kinds of thought.

127
00:09:18,213 --> 00:09:23,214
[SPEAKER_00]: So, bhikkhus, whatever a bhikkhu frequently thinks and ponders upon, that will become the inclination of his mind.

128
00:09:23,855 --> 00:09:32,097
[SPEAKER_00]: If he frequently thinks and ponders upon thoughts of sensual desire, he has abandoned the thought of renunciation, in order to cultivate the thought of sensual desire.

129
00:09:32,278 --> 00:09:34,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, his mind inclines towards sensual desire.

130
00:09:34,998 --> 00:09:45,142
[SPEAKER_00]: If he frequently thinks and ponders upon thoughts of ill-will, thoughts of cruelty, he has abandoned the thought of non-cruelty, and his mind will incline towards cruelty.

131
00:09:46,190 --> 00:09:48,971
[SPEAKER_00]: So, too, I saw the unwholesome states, danger, degradation, defilement.

132
00:09:48,991 --> 00:09:53,053
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the mind starts leaning towards the unwholesome states, that's when you poke it, hit it back.

133
00:09:53,073 --> 00:09:54,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't go there.

134
00:09:54,333 --> 00:09:55,594
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't welcome it.

135
00:10:15,421 --> 00:10:15,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

136
00:10:15,641 --> 00:10:16,402
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can think it.

137
00:10:16,422 --> 00:10:16,982
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll finish.

138
00:10:17,062 --> 00:10:17,302
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll finish.

139
00:10:41,459 --> 00:10:46,283
[SPEAKER_00]: You can think it as much as you want it, because it's within the boundaries of wholesome.

140
00:10:47,003 --> 00:10:49,365
[SPEAKER_00]: It's within the domain of renunciation.

141
00:10:49,445 --> 00:10:52,567
[SPEAKER_00]: It's non-ill-will, non-sensuality, non-cruelty.

142
00:10:53,648 --> 00:10:56,891
[SPEAKER_00]: But, with excessive thinking and pondering, I might tire my mind and body.

143
00:10:56,931 --> 00:10:58,632
[SPEAKER_00]: When the body is tired, the mind becomes strained.

144
00:10:58,652 --> 00:11:01,074
[SPEAKER_00]: When the mind is strained, it's far from composure.

145
00:11:01,794 --> 00:11:05,457
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I study my mind internally, quietly, brought to singleness, composed it.

146
00:11:05,537 --> 00:11:06,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

147
00:11:06,358 --> 00:11:07,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So that my mind should not be strained.

148
00:11:09,053 --> 00:11:14,297
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, your problem here is that you read this last bit, and now you question everything before.

149
00:11:14,677 --> 00:11:15,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

150
00:11:15,258 --> 00:11:21,582
[SPEAKER_00]: You calm your mind, once you have fully established it upon the proper thinking of the domain of renunciation.

151
00:11:22,683 --> 00:11:25,905
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, by all means, overthink renunciation.

152
00:11:26,886 --> 00:11:37,674
[SPEAKER_00]: When that get reaches fulfillment, then you will see that, oh, actually, I can carry this further, whereby I won't have to think and be equally steady and calm.

153
00:11:37,694 --> 00:11:38,154
[SPEAKER_01]: So there you...

154
00:11:39,765 --> 00:11:49,069
[SPEAKER_00]: If you develop thoughts of renunciation fully, properly, then you can start thinking about composing your mind internally, quiet it, and bring it to singleness.

155
00:11:49,269 --> 00:11:49,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Not before.

156
00:11:51,931 --> 00:11:57,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you can develop second jhāna once you have fully developed first.

157
00:11:59,514 --> 00:12:02,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, from the point of view of second jhāna, first jhāna is tiresome.

158
00:12:03,496 --> 00:12:04,036
[SPEAKER_00]: It's coarse.

159
00:12:05,417 --> 00:12:07,278
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly how the description goes.

160
00:12:08,341 --> 00:12:09,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the course falls off.

161
00:12:09,763 --> 00:12:12,446
[SPEAKER_00]: The course thinking and pondering has been subsided.

162
00:12:13,628 --> 00:12:17,032
[SPEAKER_00]: But not from Puthujjana's mind, or from a non-jhāna point of view.

163
00:12:17,052 --> 00:12:19,255
[SPEAKER_00]: The first jhāna is more than enough for arahantship.

164
00:12:20,957 --> 00:12:21,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, that's exactly.

165
00:12:21,897 --> 00:12:22,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Sutta is like this.

166
00:12:22,558 --> 00:12:24,698
[SPEAKER_00]: When people would read, you read this bit.

167
00:12:24,718 --> 00:12:25,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay.

168
00:12:25,158 --> 00:12:27,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I must stop my thinking, because that's tiresome.

169
00:12:27,679 --> 00:12:33,501
[SPEAKER_00]: That will become tiresome only once you're fully withdrawn from sensuality, from ill-will, from cruelty.

170
00:12:33,942 --> 00:12:42,825
[SPEAKER_00]: When you spend day and night thinking about renunciation, then you will realize that, actually, I don't have to do even this anymore, because it got so developed.

171
00:12:42,905 --> 00:12:47,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I can quiet it and bring the mind to that unification point.

172
00:12:48,106 --> 00:12:48,607
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

173
00:12:48,647 --> 00:12:49,387
[SPEAKER_00]: By all means,

174
00:12:50,347 --> 00:12:52,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Think day and night about renunciation.

175
00:12:53,129 --> 00:12:57,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the Buddha said himself, the thought of renunciation has arisen me.

176
00:12:57,712 --> 00:12:58,993
[SPEAKER_00]: It does not lead to affliction.

177
00:12:59,033 --> 00:13:00,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Does not lead to affliction of the others.

178
00:13:00,534 --> 00:13:01,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Does not cause difficulties.

179
00:13:02,476 --> 00:13:03,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Leads to nibbāna.

180
00:13:04,057 --> 00:13:08,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you don't need to go to this second bit that then he says, I quiet it instead it.

181
00:13:10,061 --> 00:13:12,523
[SPEAKER_00]: That in itself leads to nibbāna.

182
00:13:12,563 --> 00:13:17,026
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you also realize, I might as well go to nibbāna in an even more pleasant way.

183
00:13:18,303 --> 00:13:23,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Because this is already pleasant from the point of view of being flogged, imprisoned, blamed by sensuality.

184
00:13:23,985 --> 00:13:25,586
[SPEAKER_00]: You're free from danger of sensuality.

185
00:13:25,686 --> 00:13:26,867
[SPEAKER_00]: First jhāna is very pleasant.

186
00:13:27,367 --> 00:13:30,648
[SPEAKER_00]: But from a point of view of second jhāna and third jhāna, first jhāna is coarse.

187
00:13:30,888 --> 00:13:31,549
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very coarse.

188
00:13:32,049 --> 00:13:33,109
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not refined at all.

189
00:13:36,431 --> 00:13:41,593
[SPEAKER_00]: But failing to see that there is the order of development, it's like the the sutta of that simile of the cow.

190
00:13:42,443 --> 00:13:48,489
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like a monk starts thinking about the second jhāna before he even fully understood and developed the theme of the first jhāna.

191
00:13:48,569 --> 00:13:49,190
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he doesn't get any.

192
00:13:49,210 --> 00:13:50,251
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what happens.

193
00:13:50,271 --> 00:13:51,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, okay, so this suits my view now.

194
00:14:05,518 --> 00:14:06,439
[SPEAKER_00]: I must stop thinking.

195
00:14:06,539 --> 00:14:16,426
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you forget everything else you read beforehand, which is like seven paragraphs of talking how to think rightly, in order to arrive at the point from which you can see the value of composure without the thought.

196
00:14:17,107 --> 00:14:17,948
[SPEAKER_00]: But not before that.

197
00:14:18,868 --> 00:14:19,809
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes into jhānas.

198
00:14:21,991 --> 00:14:24,352
[SPEAKER_00]: In a few paragraphs goes exactly what I'm talking about now.

199
00:14:24,633 --> 00:14:26,474
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I wasn't... Yeah, I wasn't...

200
00:14:27,235 --> 00:14:30,717
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought it's gonna talk about different things, but actually, no, it just went the same way I just said it.

201
00:14:30,837 --> 00:14:31,578
[SPEAKER_00]: It went to jhānas.

202
00:14:32,716 --> 00:14:33,997
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't even see that.

203
00:14:34,037 --> 00:14:34,957
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

204
00:14:35,177 --> 00:14:36,098
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah.

205
00:14:36,458 --> 00:14:38,779
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I made the mistake.

206
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:42,321
[SPEAKER_00]: And the sutta is called Two Kinds of Thought.

207
00:14:44,883 --> 00:14:46,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Not two kinds of you must stop thinking.

208
00:14:49,185 --> 00:14:52,767
[SPEAKER_00]: It's right thinking and wrong thinking.

209
00:14:52,807 --> 00:14:54,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose I divide my thoughts into two classes.

210
00:14:55,769 --> 00:15:01,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, I set on one side thoughts of sensual desire, ill-will and cruelty, and on the other side thoughts of non-sensual desire,

211
00:15:02,018 --> 00:15:03,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-ill-will and non-cruelty.

212
00:15:08,022 --> 00:15:10,584
[SPEAKER_00]: You apply the effort when necessary.

213
00:15:11,084 --> 00:15:15,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you pull that bull, you poke it, you hit it with a stick.

214
00:15:16,628 --> 00:15:19,509
[SPEAKER_00]: When it goes towards sensuality, ill-will and cruelty, not when it doesn't.

215
00:15:20,570 --> 00:15:24,132
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's within non-sensuality, non-ill-will, non-cruelty, great.

216
00:15:24,953 --> 00:15:26,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Leave it, leave it, let it be.

217
00:15:26,614 --> 00:15:27,595
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it develops peace.

218
00:15:32,465 --> 00:15:36,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever I thought of sensual desire arousing me, I've done away with it.

219
00:15:39,649 --> 00:15:42,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever I thought of ill will, cruelty, I've done away with it.

220
00:15:43,631 --> 00:15:59,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you take this sutta to mean that you entered the first jhāna upon composing your mind, that's done after your mind has been fully cultivating only the wholesome domain of thought.

221
00:16:01,811 --> 00:16:13,842
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you can't even conceive how you would study the mind in the first jhāna, unless you fully abandon the unwholesome thoughts first, through thinking wholesome thoughts.

222
00:16:19,487 --> 00:16:23,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not quite clear from this, the way this translation lists it.

223
00:16:23,291 --> 00:16:26,514
[SPEAKER_00]: But either way, either way, what I said, there is the order to it.

224
00:16:26,614 --> 00:16:27,535
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same principle.

225
00:16:32,537 --> 00:16:42,863
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't start like, oh, I'm gonna compose my mind in the jhāna without having done away with unwholesome thoughts and cultivated wholesome thoughts, without basically learning how to think properly.

226
00:16:43,843 --> 00:16:47,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can start seeing what's more peaceful now within this wholesomeness.

227
00:16:48,466 --> 00:16:57,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it would be really, really peaceful if the bull would just completely sit down and stop walking even within the allowed perimeter of walking, within my own ground.

228
00:16:58,191 --> 00:16:59,372
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you bring it down.

229
00:17:00,063 --> 00:17:01,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can say that's the first jhāna.

230
00:17:01,684 --> 00:17:05,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can abandon thinking about that bull, that would be the second jhāna, and so on.

231
00:17:05,946 --> 00:17:06,466
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

232
00:17:06,546 --> 00:17:22,213
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, you can't start composing your mind properly, unifying your thought, without having learning... without having to learn... without learning how to think properly beforehand.

233
00:17:25,954 --> 00:17:27,515
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you have to spend day and night

234
00:17:28,377 --> 00:17:32,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Cultivating thoughts of renunciation, non-ill-will, non-cruelty.

235
00:17:32,220 --> 00:17:39,146
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, that's in itself, from the point of view of ill-will, sensuality and cruelty, that is effortless.

236
00:17:40,888 --> 00:17:46,973
[SPEAKER_00]: The only time you need to make the effort is when there is the prospect of sensuality, ill-will and cruelty.

237
00:17:47,173 --> 00:17:52,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you make the effort to not delight in it, not welcome it, not entertain further thoughts upon it.

238
00:17:53,518 --> 00:17:56,161
[SPEAKER_00]: But, whenever that effort is needed, you do it.

239
00:17:57,233 --> 00:17:58,793
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what means diligent and ardent.

240
00:17:58,893 --> 00:18:01,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you do the work when work is necessary.

241
00:18:01,274 --> 00:18:05,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Three o'clock at night, three o'clock at night, four o'clock in the afternoon, four... You don't say, I'll do it later.

242
00:18:05,615 --> 00:18:06,935
[SPEAKER_00]: That means you're not diligent.

243
00:18:07,875 --> 00:18:10,036
[SPEAKER_00]: But people would usually think diligent means every second.

244
00:18:10,236 --> 00:18:10,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Every second.

245
00:18:10,856 --> 00:18:11,936
[SPEAKER_00]: One, two, three, four.

246
00:18:12,016 --> 00:18:13,817
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm watching, I'm watching, I'm watching, I'm watching.

247
00:18:14,017 --> 00:18:14,997
[SPEAKER_00]: No, there's nothing to watch.

248
00:18:15,337 --> 00:18:15,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't watch it.

249
00:18:16,798 --> 00:18:18,898
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no sensuality, no ill will, no cruelty.

250
00:18:19,518 --> 00:18:23,459
[SPEAKER_00]: You see it when it arises, because it's always on you to accept it or

251
00:18:28,873 --> 00:18:33,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from that point of view, renunciation, not cruelty, not ill will, is effortless.

252
00:18:33,916 --> 00:18:36,998
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't require any effort to not engage with sensuality.

253
00:18:38,919 --> 00:18:39,839
[SPEAKER_00]: That's actually default.

254
00:18:40,560 --> 00:18:41,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality requires effort.

255
00:18:43,201 --> 00:18:44,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill will requires effort.

256
00:18:44,902 --> 00:18:45,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Cruelty requires effort.

257
00:18:48,684 --> 00:18:52,226
[SPEAKER_00]: But because people never understood that, they don't see it like that.

258
00:18:53,427 --> 00:18:55,568
[SPEAKER_00]: They think renunciation is the painful.

259
00:18:56,432 --> 00:18:56,792
[SPEAKER_00]: aspect.

260
00:18:57,192 --> 00:18:58,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no hurts.

261
00:18:58,473 --> 00:18:59,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no requires effort.

262
00:19:00,733 --> 00:19:01,213
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't.

263
00:19:01,593 --> 00:19:06,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no reveals the pain of that which you indulge with, so you don't... so you master the pain.

264
00:19:07,455 --> 00:19:08,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Denunciation is completely out of it.

265
00:19:10,895 --> 00:19:19,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you fully develop that, which, as I said, it's peaceful from the point of view of sensuality, then you can ask yourself, oh, can it be even more peaceful?

266
00:19:23,398 --> 00:19:24,059
[SPEAKER_00]: But only then.

267
00:19:24,499 --> 00:19:25,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

268
00:19:25,539 --> 00:19:25,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

269
00:19:26,919 --> 00:19:28,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you'll be just acting out of that sensuality.

270
00:19:38,062 --> 00:19:44,923
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to train an animal, you have to stop trying to control it, whatever it does.

271
00:19:44,983 --> 00:19:49,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Only control it, pull it back, when it does things that are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

272
00:19:49,324 --> 00:19:51,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with the dogs here.

273
00:19:51,405 --> 00:19:55,726
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to shout at them all the time, they simply won't know what they're supposed to do.

274
00:19:56,424 --> 00:20:01,745
[SPEAKER_00]: How can then your mind learn what you want it to do?

275
00:20:04,486 --> 00:20:09,407
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean when I keep saying to allow your mind to think, allow things to rise, to manifest.

276
00:20:17,349 --> 00:20:22,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Let them endure, but keep a corner of your eye as in

277
00:20:24,210 --> 00:20:26,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there sensuality arising from this or not?

278
00:20:26,332 --> 00:20:28,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you just, again, you don't deny it, that it has arisen.

279
00:20:29,495 --> 00:20:30,395
[SPEAKER_00]: You just draw a line.

280
00:20:30,596 --> 00:20:31,256
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't go there.

281
00:20:33,118 --> 00:20:34,159
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not entertain this.

282
00:20:34,439 --> 00:20:35,460
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not welcome this.

283
00:20:38,682 --> 00:20:48,070
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, eh... losing the sight of it, like... Well, even the losing of the sight can be only lost on account of decisions that you made.

284
00:20:48,130 --> 00:20:49,772
[SPEAKER_00]: They were rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

285
00:20:51,366 --> 00:20:52,667
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why mindfulness, you're effortless.

286
00:20:53,067 --> 00:20:55,428
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why ignorance is always comes down to your choice.

287
00:20:56,048 --> 00:20:56,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorant choice.

288
00:20:57,469 --> 00:21:01,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you stop making ignorant choices, mindfulness becomes unshakable.

289
00:21:03,811 --> 00:21:06,752
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not mindful when you're thinking I'm mindful, I'm mindful, I'm mindful.

290
00:21:07,112 --> 00:21:10,674
[SPEAKER_00]: You're mindful when your experience is free from greed, aversion, delusion.

291
00:21:11,354 --> 00:21:12,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Which includes anxiety.

292
00:21:12,695 --> 00:21:13,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

293
00:21:13,635 --> 00:21:14,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

294
00:21:14,836 --> 00:21:17,997
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why that's the root of all the problem and the solution to all the problem.

295
00:21:20,587 --> 00:21:23,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Pristine awareness and purity of your concentration.

296
00:21:24,490 --> 00:21:25,970
[SPEAKER_00]: All that is just wishful thinking.

297
00:21:26,691 --> 00:21:29,913
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove greed, aversion, delusion, you are automatically mindful.

298
00:21:30,053 --> 00:21:31,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't call it mindfulness.

299
00:21:31,454 --> 00:21:32,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Call it whatever you want.

300
00:21:33,895 --> 00:21:35,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Means the clarity is there, basically.

301
00:21:39,178 --> 00:21:42,581
[SPEAKER_01]: This doubt in itself, anxiety in itself, are decisions.

302
00:21:44,012 --> 00:21:49,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they might manifest themselves on their own, but then you decide to engage, always.

303
00:21:50,077 --> 00:21:50,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with doubt.

304
00:21:51,178 --> 00:21:53,580
[SPEAKER_00]: You are not strong enough to not engage with doubt.

305
00:21:56,993 --> 00:22:00,476
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, you can't just say... tell yourself, I will not engage with this.

306
00:22:00,917 --> 00:22:02,178
[SPEAKER_00]: By default, you'll engage with it.

307
00:22:02,759 --> 00:22:10,486
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to start including that initial engagement in that doubt, in that mindfulness, as belonging to that doubt, not belonging to you.

308
00:22:11,267 --> 00:22:16,251
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you can, at any time, at any part of that engaged doubt, you can step outside of it.

309
00:22:16,331 --> 00:22:19,935
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you can abandon hindrance perpendicularly at any given point.

310
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:27,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Included in the arisen phenomena, there you are mindful of.

311
00:22:27,901 --> 00:22:30,522
[SPEAKER_00]: And then that, what you stop entertaining and fueling.

312
00:22:38,304 --> 00:22:38,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you have a choice.

313
00:22:39,624 --> 00:22:43,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Shall I stay then with this general, general context, general picture?

314
00:22:44,145 --> 00:22:47,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Or shall I give in and attend to the aspect of the doubt that wants me to think further about it?

315
00:22:49,807 --> 00:22:50,447
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you choose.

316
00:22:51,027 --> 00:22:53,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the content can only so much overpower you, like...

317
00:22:54,712 --> 00:22:56,753
[SPEAKER_00]: It overpowers you if you let it.

318
00:22:57,613 --> 00:23:01,395
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like somebody coming in front of your door.

319
00:23:03,636 --> 00:23:05,817
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't come in unless you let them in.

320
00:23:06,557 --> 00:23:14,861
[SPEAKER_00]: They can be shouting there, trying to provoke you from the outside, but you're the one who then chooses to step outside and get engaged with them, or invite them in and get engaged with them.

321
00:23:15,501 --> 00:23:16,601
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't do it by themselves.

322
00:23:17,122 --> 00:23:20,603
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you recognize that, that's where the confidence arises from.

323
00:23:20,623 --> 00:23:22,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can have the whole world shouting at you.

324
00:23:24,152 --> 00:23:25,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Trying to make you doubt.

325
00:23:26,014 --> 00:23:27,655
[SPEAKER_00]: But you know where the doubt is originating.

326
00:23:27,675 --> 00:23:28,395
[SPEAKER_00]: You understood it.

327
00:23:28,435 --> 00:23:30,017
[SPEAKER_00]: You went beyond its nature.

328
00:23:30,057 --> 00:23:33,500
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's said Sotāpanna can have the whole world saying the Buddha was wrong.

329
00:23:33,900 --> 00:23:34,981
[SPEAKER_00]: He will know that they are wrong.

330
00:23:36,982 --> 00:23:40,145
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, from Putujana's point of view, that just sounds like religious fundamentalism.

331
00:23:40,625 --> 00:23:41,746
[SPEAKER_00]: But in a way, it is.

332
00:23:41,786 --> 00:23:42,927
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the right fundamentalism.

333
00:23:43,388 --> 00:23:46,990
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fundamentalism that's according to the universal principles.

334
00:23:47,010 --> 00:23:47,931
[SPEAKER_00]: It's according to the dhamma.

335
00:23:48,552 --> 00:23:50,153
[SPEAKER_00]: And by all means, everybody should develop it.

